- Summary:
  - Adversaries favor abuse of legitimate tools, trusted platforms, and default workflows to access, move,...',
    '- Headline: AI-enabled and tool-abuse–driven malware and vulnerability trends define H1 2026, emphasizing normal-system evasion and broader exploitation across vendors and platforms.
- Summary:
  - Adversaries favor abuse of legitimate tools, trusted platforms, and default workflows to access, move, and monetize intrusions.
  - AI-enabled malware activity augmented existing intrusion tradecraft, mainly at low-to-mid AIM3 levels ( Levels 1–3).
  - 215 actively exploited CVEs in H1 2026; 142 were network-accessible without authentication; 60 enabled RCE.
  - RATs remained prominent; AsyncRAT, Cobalt Strike, XWorm, Stealc, REMCOS persisted in top rankings.
  - Mobile focus: Android NFC malware (NFCShare, NGate) stole data and facilitated fraud; supply-chain and third-party risks rise.
  - Defenders should emphasize exposure management, credential governance, behavioral detection, and third-party oversight.
  - Countries: # | # (example placeholders removed) 
  - Hashtags: #CyberSecurity #ThreatIntelligence #AI #Malware #VulnerabilityManagement #H12026
